{
  "version": "code-parity-v2",
  "date": "2026-09-11",
  "repository": "/home/user/llm-wiki",
  "notes": [
    "Every gold answer was established by hand from the source on 2026-08-28,",
    "by grep plus reading the cited lines — not by trusting either tool.",
    "Citations are file:line in this repository at working-tree state",
    "(branch work, after commit 406ec41; the tree also carried uncommitted",
    "additions by concurrent agents, none of which touch the cited lines).",
    "Grading terms match on word boundaries ([A-Za-z0-9_] is a word char),",
    "so 'retrieve' does not match 'retrieval'.",
    "A must entry may be a list of alternates: any one of them counts. Used",
    "where a tool legitimately answers with the call-site line instead of the",
    "enclosing function name (llm-wiki's caller edges carry file:line only);",
    "the alternate line numbers are the cited call-site lines. A bare number",
    "can in principle collide with an unrelated number in the answer; the",
    "per-task answer text is stored so any such collision is auditable.",
    "A side-level 'must' override exists only where the two tools express",
    "the same fact in incompatible vocabularies (dead = empty candidate list",
    "on one side, 'callers_total: 0' on the other); the fact graded is the",
    "same and is named in the gold citations.",
    "llm_wiki_best is the same product answered through the surface that serves each question (mostly the 2026-08-28 query mode). The gold is unchanged; only the call list differs. T05 and T12 carry no better surface and repeat the configured call so the refusal is measured, not hidden. For T06/T07 the second call carries a side-level must of '\"nodes\": []' so an empty caller frontier — not the mere echo of the symbol name — is what earns the grade.",
    "2026-08-29, anchors added to T06, T07 and T13. No question and no gold moved; only three calls that were failing to ask the question their task states. T13 was graded 'wrong' on all three sides by the 2026-08-29 run because it asked which community holds fuse_rrf without naming fuse_rrf, and scripts/code_graph.py::_communities_holding records why no whole-graph listing can answer that here. T06/T07 asked whether one function is dead by requesting every dead-code candidate in the repository, of which 307 of 846 were cut from the answer to fit the client ceiling — so a name's absence meant nothing. Each side is anchored through its own best surface for the question: get_architecture mode=community symbol=... and find_dead_code symbol=... on the llm_wiki side, search_graph on the cbm side for T13, and cbm's already-anchored trace_path unchanged for T06/T07. Both sides are rerun and reported together; see docs/research/2026-08-29-what-the-caller-asked.md.",
    "2026-09-07, a third-party side added: trace-mcp 3.22.0 (MIT), pinned, called over MCP stdio by benchmark/mcp_stdio_client.py because it has no CLI mode. Its call list was written from its own tools/list and input schemas, one call per task through the tool whose description names that task, two for T05 where a constant is neither a symbol nor only text. T13 asks for a module community, a notion trace-mcp does not have; the call is its nearest surface and the refusal is measured, not hidden — the same treatment llm_wiki_best gets on T05 and T12. Graphify is not a side: no self-hostable source exists and measuring it would mean sending the repository to a service. See docs/research/2026-09-07-which-competitors-can-actually-be-measured.md.",
    "2026-09-07, a fourth side: Serena (MIT), pinned to its git head, started with the dashboard, log window and browser off and called only through its read tools — the runner refuses any other, because Serena also ships replace_content, rename_symbol and safe_delete_symbol and a benchmark that called one of those would be editing the repository it measures. Serena is an LSP proxy with no callee tool, no call graph and no notion of a module community, so T03, T10 and T13 are answered through its nearest surface and whatever it says is graded as it stands. T10 is its only two-call task, one hop per call, which is the shape the question asks for.",
    "2026-09-11, v2. The thirteen v1 tasks are carried over unchanged, and three questions are added that v1 could not ask: two about argument bindings (the `data_flow` mode added for issue #24, B3) and one about which tests exercise a function. Gold for T14-T16 was established by hand from the working tree on 2026-09-11 at the cited lines.",
    "T14 and T15 grade llm-wiki on the exact `argument->parameter` pairs it prints, and every other side on the caller-visible names alone, because no two tools spell a binding the same way. The fact graded is the same and is named in the gold citations.",
    "Cross-service questions are not in this file: this repository serves no HTTP route, so a cross-service answer here would be vacuous. They live in `benchmark/code-parity-cross-service-v1.json`, against the two-repository fixture `benchmark/build_cross_service_fixture.py` builds."
  ],
